    {
      "id": "v1/5nn4_SC2_A_1017__top2/r01/G1",
      "evidence_sha256": "8b0b10eedbf6217d83d2ba7f24b81fd8536a9832337ddc1a78986c484c737970",
      "assessment": "R1 G1 correctly reports Mean +0.054551 and Q2/Q3 gains, but reads the reverse-query 48% win rate and -3.535 minimum as treatment statistics; the treatment largest gain is +3.535 and its win rate is 52%. It claims another 6-to-7 tick nearly doubles total displacement and uses unguided-prefix switching as early-guided evidence. H1 [7,7,5,5] is selected and falls to 4.075670; H2 duplicates the selected [6,6,6,6] from G3.",
      "decision_update": "Normalize sign, scale and interval labels before mechanistic inference. A 5-to-7 nominal ratio is 1.4, not two, absent measured nonlinear effects. Keep the useful observed concentration analysis and cautious two-mechanism design without requiring more than half of candidates to win for Mean to improve.",
      "counterexample_or_limitation": "The further early extension loses Mean despite increased displacement, contradicting the simple scaling forecast. The balanced schedule succeeds modestly but has a different proposal source and selected restart parents; success does not validate the early-versus-late causal story.",
